WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.610
Film Courage: Do you have a routine when starting
a screenplay, whether it’s a feature-length

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.480
or a short?

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.240
David Willis: I often write backwards.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:16.120
I often think of an ending and then I think
“What’s a fun way to get there.”

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.390
So I often do that and then I think about
it.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:21.672
I think about it a lot.

00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:26.390
I just let it boil around in there for awhile.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.070
And then I outline it.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.769
Outlining, getting the story right, that’s
the hardest part.

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:37.760
Once you’ve got all that down, once you’ve
got the story working and the characters working,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.620
then it’s just easy to write.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.359
Half the stuff I’ve sold I’ve written
with partners.

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:49.160
Comedy is great to write with a partner because
you can work off of each other.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.839
You can make a suggestion and then you play
off of each other’s pitches.

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:57.309
And then you arrive at something that neither
of you would have gotten to alone that’s

00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:58.309
better.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:02.250
So when you’re working in comedy with a
writing partner, it speeds it up.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.420
You are like a draft or two ahead of what
you would have been if you doing it alone.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.329
Film Courage: What are your best screenwriting
habits and then what are your worst screenwriting

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:12.910
habits (and you don’t have to give me five
of them.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:16.329
Just one or two.)?

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:24.250
David Willis: My best screenwriting habits…you
know…I hate to say this, but there is no

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.070
like magic habit or thing.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.110
You just put your self in the chair and you
just write.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:38.380
It’s a job, so whether you feel like it
or not you sit and you do your job.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.930
I mean, there’s no magic.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:46.799
I’ve had some young writers say to me “Oh,
I’m going on this writing retreat for a

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:49.520
weekend so I can finally work on my script!”

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.090
And I’m like “…Uh…I’ve never been
on a retreat to write.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.969
I will just write, there’s nothing magical
about it.

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:03.280
Sure maybe the first draft will be bad stuff
but if you don’t write the first draft,

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.999
you won’t write the second draft.”

00:02:05.999 --> 00:02:07.320
You just have to demystify it.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.250
Sometimes people just build it up into this
big magical thing…demystification…just

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.250
do it!

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:21.670
In my own writing, the last thing I put in,
is visual comedy and set pieces because that’s

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:27.830
not where my mind goes first and I have to
remind myself, make this visual, something

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.840
physical, a big set piece where crazy, big
stuff happens.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.230
And that’s just something that it’s the
last thing I do because I don’t think about

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.230
it first.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.720
So that’s all, I just have to remind myself
“Oh, we could use something big here…”

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.720
and then I put it in.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.640
Film Courage: And is that your worst screenwriting
habit?

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:47.710
David Willis: That’s my worst.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:48.780
Film Courage: That’s your worst?

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:49.780
Okay.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:50.780
David Willis: That’s my worst.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:57.900
It’s like a weakness that is not like there
from the start.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.980
It’s like when you leave the house and you
go “Oh…did I leave the stove on?”

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.860
I have to be “Did I put in some physical
comedy?

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:07.860
No…okay?”

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.340
That’s all.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.490
I mean there is nothing mysterious about it.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.150
You just write, write your script.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:15.650
You can’t teach writing.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:16.760
You’re either a writer or you’re not.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.700
You can teach technique, you can teach craft,
you can teach how to fix certain problems.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.750
But if you’re a writer, you are writing
everyday and if you don’t have time everyday,

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:27.610
you’re thinking about it.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:32.090
Film Courage: For the new writers though,
don’t you think they almost need a ritual?

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.370
I know this retreat idea (for someone who
has written) sounds like why would you need

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.370
all of that?

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.870
Why would you need to fly there [to the retreat
location] and pay money or whatever but maybe

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:45.090
some people need that ritual…no?

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.090
In churches they have rituals.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.990
Christmas time (is a form of a ritual).

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:50.990
No?

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.540
David Willis: The only ritual is sit down
and write.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.450
That’s the only ritual.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.570
There’s nothing else to it.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:00.880
There really isn’t.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.890
I mean I wish there was some kind of magical…and
people also ask me “Gee, what book should

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:05.890
I read on screenwriting?

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:06.890
I’m just starting to write.”

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:07.890
[And I say] “Don’t read a book!”

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:08.890
Film Courage: No?

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:09.890
David Willis: No!

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:10.890
Don’t read a book.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.000
Write 5 or 6 scripts, then read a book because
then if we read a book now it’s abstract,

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.000
it’s theoretical.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.870
You haven’t done it, okay?

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.710
That’s like saying “What sheet music should
I buy?”

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.780
“Do you have an instrument?”

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.780
“No?”

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.020
“Pick up an instrument, start playing it
first, okay?”

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.020
Because the books will make no sense.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:33.950
I’ve wrote like 5 or 6…I sold scripts
before I ever read any books on screenwriting

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:39.670
and then I got [the book] Story by Robert
McKee and I’m like “Oh?

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:41.670
That’s interesting.”

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.460
Because I had a problem in a script and I
couldn’t figure out how to solve it and

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.880
I was reading it and I go “Oh?

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.080
That’s how I could fix it.”

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.770
Now if I’d read that before I had the problem
then that particular chapter would have made

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.970
no sense to me because I didn’t have that
problem yet because I hadn’t written enough.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.060
I always say to these new writers when they
are talking to me and about getting new books

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.530
and doing things like that, I say “How many
scripts have you written?”

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:09.840
And if they answer me with a number and if
they know how many scripts they’ve written,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.030
they haven’t written enough scripts.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.062
You shouldn’t know how many scripts you’ve
written.

00:05:14.062 --> 00:05:18.500
You should be writing so many scripts that
you’d have to think about it for a minute

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.200
and figure out “Oh…I wrote this and then
that…”

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.790
If you know how many drafts exactly you did
or something, you haven’t done enough drafts.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:32.490
Once you do so much of this, you build the
muscles, you just keep writing, writing, writing…you

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:38.150
build up the writing muscles and then you
don’t need rituals, you don’t need this

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:42.090
stuff because you built it up, you can do
it.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:46.490
Film Courage: You said earlier about writers
under [age] 30 that they really hadn’t lived

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.180
life (and I’m sure it would depend on the
individual).

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.180
David Willis: Yes.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.350
Film Courage: I mean I’m sure there are
people who could have lived [experienced a

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:54.290
very diverse existence] by the time they are
15 and for others, even by the time they are

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.930
age 50, everything has been safe and easy
and taken care of for them.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.220
But what are some things [they can do] where
they can live life?

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:02.200
Let’s suppose [this hypothetical writer
under age 30] has just a “normal” life

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:08.670
with some good and some bad, regular ups and
downs, how can they live life to get sort

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:09.670
of a writerly-brain?

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:10.670
David Willis: [Laughs]

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:14.900
Film Courage: I know that is a really bad
term, we’re getting toward the end here

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:15.900
[of the interview with David].

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.530
But what I mean is so that they can become
a little more well-rounded by 30 or whatever

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.530
age.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.350
It doesn’t matter what age, I guess?

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:26.260
David Willis: You just have to be around people.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:32.190
You have to have friends and romances and
things like that and get your heart broken

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:42.540
and break some hearts and don’t sit at home
watching…don’t go to work your day job,

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.290
come home and watch TV for 6 hours and go
to bed.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.810
Go to Meetups, go do stuff, meet people because
that’s what matters.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.830
I thought I was some weirdo who like doing
this, but I found out that other people (other

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:57.000
writers) like to do this which is if you’re
in a cafe or whatever, eavesdrop on the people

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:03.330
around you because you are hearing real dialogue
and real stuff and it’s really illuminating.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:08.250
I don’t know, that’s the only advice I
can offer it so just get the heck out of your

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.730
apartment.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.560
Get off your futon, go live life!

